QB.push(
{id:1991,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"MCP carries its messages as JSON-RPC 2.0. Which structural difference distinguishes a JSON-RPC *notification* from a JSON-RPC *request* on an MCP connection?",
o:["A. A notification sets `jsonrpc` to `1.0` instead of `2.0`","B. A notification carries its payload in `result` rather than `params`","C. A notification carries no `id` member, so no response is ever returned for it","D. A notification must travel on a separate connection from requests"],a:2,
e:"In **JSON-RPC 2.0** a request has an `id` that correlates it with exactly one response; a notification omits `id` entirely and is fire-and-forget. MCP uses notifications for one-way traffic: `notifications/progress`, `notifications/cancelled`, `notifications/tools/list_changed`, `notifications/resources/updated`. The transports make this concrete — on Streamable HTTP a notification POST that the server accepts is answered with `202 Accepted` and no body, whereas a request POST returns either a JSON object or an SSE stream ending in the response. The **transferable rule**: if you need an answer, you need an `id`.",
w:"A — every MCP message declares `jsonrpc` as `2.0`; there is no 1.0 mode. B — `result` appears in responses, not in outbound requests or notifications; both carry `params`. D — both share one channel: on stdio a single stdout stream, on Streamable HTTP the same MCP endpoint."},

{id:1992,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A Python MCP server runs over stdio. During debugging a developer adds `print(f\"loaded {n} records\")` to the startup path. The host now reports a parse failure on the first line it reads from the server and drops the connection. Nothing else changed.\n\nWhat is the cause and the correct fix?",
o:["A. Output on stdio must be base64-encoded; the plain-text line broke the encoding","B. On stdio, everything the server writes to `stdout` must be a valid MCP message; diagnostics belong on `stderr`, which the host captures separately","C. stdio cannot carry log output at all, so the server must move to Streamable HTTP","D. The host should be configured to skip lines on `stdout` that fail to parse"],a:1,
e:"The **stdio** binding is newline-delimited JSON-RPC over a client-launched subprocess: the server reads messages from `stdin` and writes messages to `stdout`, one per line, with no embedded newlines. The spec is explicit that the server **MUST NOT** write anything to `stdout` that is not a valid MCP message. `stderr` is the sanctioned log channel — the server MAY write UTF-8 there for any purpose, and the client MAY capture, forward or ignore it and SHOULD NOT treat output there as an error. So `print()` to stdout corrupts the frame; `logging` to stderr does not.",
w:"A — stdio frames are plain UTF-8 JSON text, not base64; encoding is not the issue. C — stdio supports logging fine, just on `stderr`; note that under Streamable HTTP the client does not capture `stderr` at all, so you would need your own aggregation. D — silently discarding unparseable lines hides real protocol bugs and is not what the spec asks of clients; fix the server."},

{id:1993,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"How is the target of `resources/read` identified, compared with the targets of `tools/call` and `prompts/get`?",
o:["A. All three take an integer index into the corresponding `*/list` result","B. All three take a `name`; resources additionally require a `mimeType`","C. `resources/read` takes a `name`, while `tools/call` and `prompts/get` take a `uri`","D. `resources/read` takes a `uri`, while `tools/call` and `prompts/get` take a `name` plus `arguments`"],a:3,
e:"Resources are **addressed by URI** — every resource is uniquely identified by an RFC 3986 URI, and `resources/read` takes `{ \"uri\": ... }`. Tools and prompts are addressed by **name**: `tools/call` takes `{ \"name\", \"arguments\" }` and `prompts/get` takes `{ \"name\", \"arguments\" }`. The asymmetry is deliberate: a URI is a stable, shareable handle for a piece of *content* that a host can bookmark, embed as a `resource_link`, or hand to a picker, while a name identifies an *action* or a template you invoke with parameters.",
w:"A — list results are paginated with an opaque `cursor` and the set may change between calls, so positional indexes would be meaningless. B — resources are keyed by `uri`, not `name`; `mimeType` is optional descriptive metadata, never the identifier. C — this inverts the two: it is resources that use a URI."},

{id:1994,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An MCP server exposes each customer's latest invoice as a resource so that users can pick one from the host's resource browser. With 80,000 customers, `resources/list` returns pages the user cannot realistically scroll, and the server spends most of its time enumerating.\n\nWhat is the right way to model this?",
o:["A. Expose a resource template with a `uriTemplate` such as `invoice://{customer_id}/latest`, discoverable through `resources/templates/list`, and let the client construct the concrete URI","B. Return all 80,000 entries and rely on the client to page through them with `cursor`","C. Abandon resources for a `get_invoice` tool, since resources cannot be parameterised","D. Keep one static `uri` and encode the customer id in the resource `name`"],a:0,
e:"**Resource templates** exist precisely for parameterised address spaces. The server advertises an RFC 6570 `uriTemplate` via `resources/templates/list`; the client expands it into a concrete URI and calls `resources/read`. Template arguments can be auto-completed through the completion API, so the picker stays usable without enumerating the space. The **principle**: enumerate what is small and browsable, template what is large and addressable. Nothing about templates changes `resources/read` — the server still receives an ordinary `uri`.",
w:"B — pagination bounds the response size but not the total, and it does not make an 80,000-item picker usable; enumeration remains the wrong shape. C — resources are parameterisable via templates, and the requirement is explicitly that the user browse and select, which is the resource control model rather than a model-invoked tool. D — the `uri` is the identifier, so collapsing 80,000 resources onto one URI makes them unaddressable; `name` is display metadata."},

{id:1995,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An MCP server answers `resources/read` for a URI that does not exist by returning `{ \"contents\": [] }`. The agent tells the user \"the onboarding document is empty\" and moves on. The document actually exists under a different URI.\n\nWhy is the server's response wrong?",
o:["A. `contents` must never be empty, so the server should return a single entry whose `text` is the empty string","B. The server should have set `isError: true` on the read result","C. A non-existent resource must be reported as a JSON-RPC error (`-32602`); an empty `contents` array is ambiguous between \"exists but has no content\" and \"does not exist\"","D. The server should return HTTP 404 and let the client translate it into a not-found result"],a:2,
e:"The spec states that servers **MUST NOT** return an empty `contents` array for a non-existent resource, for exactly the reason the scenario shows: the array is ambiguous, and the agent resolves the ambiguity in the least useful direction. A missing resource is a JSON-RPC error with code **`-32602`** (Invalid params), optionally carrying the offending `uri` in `data`; clients should also accept `-32002` for backward compatibility, since earlier revisions used it. Internal faults use `-32603`. The **transferable principle**: never encode \"not found\" as a successful empty result.",
w:"A — an empty-string `text` asserts the resource exists and is blank, which is the same false statement the agent already made. B — `isError` is a field of a *tool* result, not of a resource read; resource failures are signalled as JSON-RPC errors. D — the HTTP status is a transport detail that does not exist on stdio, and it would not carry the JSON-RPC error the client needs; protocol semantics are identical on every binding."},

{id:1996,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"The specification describes MCP's three server primitives as having different intended control models. Which mapping is correct?",
o:["A. Tools are user-controlled, resources are model-controlled, prompts are application-driven","B. Tools are model-controlled, prompts are user-controlled, resources are application-driven","C. All three are model-controlled; the host merely decides whether to surface them","D. Tools are application-driven, prompts are model-controlled, resources are user-controlled"],a:1,
e:"**Tools are model-controlled** — the model discovers them via `tools/list` and decides when to invoke `tools/call`, which is why tools are the *action* primitive and why hosts are told to keep a human able to deny an invocation. **Prompts are user-controlled**: they are exposed so the user can explicitly select them, typically as slash commands. Note carefully what this means — it is about who decides *when* a prompt is used, not who authors it; the server still owns the content. **Resources are application-driven**: the host decides how to incorporate that content, whether by a picker, a search, or automatic inclusion. The action-versus-content split maps onto tools versus resources; prompts are user-triggered workflows.",
w:"A — inverts tools and prompts, which would hide a state-changing action behind a user-triggered workflow the model cannot reach on its own initiative. C — flattening all three onto model control erases the point of the split: resources exist so the host can decide what content enters context, and prompts so the user can invoke a workflow deliberately. D — scrambles all three, and putting browsable reference content behind a model-controlled primitive forces the model to fetch what the user could simply have selected."},

{id:1997,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team is rolling out a reworked `incident_triage` MCP prompt. To let clients stay on the old wording during the transition, they add `\"version\": \"2\"` to the prompt definition returned by `prompts/list` and expect clients to request version 1 until they migrate. Every client ignores the field and receives the new wording.\n\nWhat did they misunderstand?",
o:["A. The field must be named `protocolVersion` for clients to recognise it","B. Prompt versioning only takes effect once the server also declares `prompts.listChanged`","C. Prompt versions are negotiated through `server/discover`, so they must appear in the server's advertised capabilities","D. The prompt primitive has no protocol-level versioning field: a prompt is identified by `name` alone, so distinct versions need distinct names (or a documented argument), and the user selects between them explicitly"],a:3,
e:"A prompt definition carries `name`, optional `title`, `description`, `icons` and `arguments` — and **no versioning field**. Unknown members are not negotiated, so an invented `version` key is inert. Because prompts are **user-controlled**, the clean migration is to make the choice visible where the user makes it: publish `incident_triage` and `incident_triage_v2` as separate names (or add an explicit argument), let both resolve, and retire the old name after a deprecation window. **Principle:** version an MCP interface through its identifiers, not through fields the protocol does not define.",
w:"A — the reserved metadata keys are the prefixed `_meta.io.modelcontextprotocol/*` fields; there is no `protocolVersion` slot inside a prompt definition, and the protocol version is the wire revision, not the prompt's revision. B — `listChanged` only says whether the server will notify subscribers that the prompt list changed; it selects nothing. C — `server/discover` returns supported protocol versions, capabilities and server identity, not per-prompt versions."},

{id:1998,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An MCP `reindex_catalog` tool takes 20 to 40 minutes. The current implementation blocks the `tools/call` until the work finishes. It passes on a developer laptop but in production the corporate proxy tears the connection down after 60 seconds, and if the desktop client restarts mid-run the user has no way to learn the outcome.\n\nWhich property of the Tasks extension addresses both symptoms?",
o:["A. The server returns a durable `taskId` immediately, so no connection is held open for the duration and the client can resume polling `tasks/get` with the same id after a reconnect or restart","B. Tasks compress the response so it fits inside the proxy's buffer","C. Tasks upgrade the transport to a keep-alive SSE stream that intermediaries will not terminate","D. Tasks automatically re-run the operation whenever the client reconnects"],a:0,
e:"The **Tasks** extension replaces a blocking request with a **durable handle**. The server creates the task durably *before* responding, then returns a `CreateTaskResult` (discriminated by a `resultType` of `task`) carrying the `taskId`, initial status, a TTL and a suggested poll interval. That directly fixes both failures the scenario names: no long-lived connection for a proxy to kill, and crash resilience because the id survives the client process. When the status reaches `completed`, `tasks/get` returns in its `result` field exactly what the synchronous call would have returned.",
w:"B — payload size is irrelevant; the proxy killed an idle connection, and Tasks defines no compression. C — Tasks does the opposite of holding a stream open: it lets the connection close between polls. Keep-alive comments exist for long-lived `subscriptions/listen` streams, and no transport can promise an intermediary will never disconnect. D — re-running a 40-minute reindex on every reconnect is a runaway, not a recovery; the client resumes *polling*, and the server keeps the single execution."},

{id:1999,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which set of JSON-RPC methods does the MCP Tasks extension define?",
o:["A. `tasks/create`, `tasks/poll`, `tasks/finish`","B. `tasks/get`, `tasks/result`, `tasks/cancel`","C. `tasks/get`, `tasks/update`, `tasks/cancel`","D. `tasks/list`, `tasks/get`, `tasks/result`, `tasks/cancel`"],a:2,
e:"The extension defines exactly three methods: **`tasks/get`** to poll state, **`tasks/update`** to supply `inputResponses` when a task is `input_required`, and **`tasks/cancel`** to request cooperative cancellation. There is no creation method — the server decides per request to return a `CreateTaskResult` instead of the normal result. **Currency note:** older material lists a blocking `tasks/result` and a `tasks/list`; both were removed in the stateless redesign that consolidated retrieval into `tasks/get` (the final payload arrives in that response's `result` field, or `error` on failure) and now answer `-32601`. Optional `notifications/tasks` push updates to clients that opted in via `subscriptions/listen`.",
w:"A — invented names; creation is not a method call at all, since the server returns a task handle from the original request. B — `tasks/result` is exactly the removed blocking method; the result comes back from `tasks/get`. D — compounds both errors: neither `tasks/list` nor `tasks/result` exists in the current extension, which is deliberate because a stateless protocol has no server-side notion of \"this client's tasks\"."},

{id:2000,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A client polls a long-running MCP task and receives a `tasks/get` response with status `input_required` and an `inputRequests` map containing an `elicitation/create` request asking the user to confirm a destructive step.\n\nWhat must the client do to let the task proceed?",
o:["A. Call `tasks/cancel`, then restart the operation with the confirmation supplied up front as a tool argument","B. Present the request to the user and send `tasks/update` with the answers in `inputResponses`, keyed to the outstanding entries in `inputRequests`","C. Open a second connection so the server can deliver the elicitation as its own JSON-RPC request","D. Re-issue the original `tools/call` with the confirmation appended to `arguments`"],a:1,
e:"`input_required` is the mid-flight interaction state. The `tasks/get` response surfaces an **`inputRequests`** map; the client gathers the input and answers with **`tasks/update`**, sending `inputResponses` under the same keys. The server acknowledges with an empty result and ignores responses for unknown or already-satisfied keys. The design point is that this needs **no second connection and no unsolicited server-to-client request** — in the current revision servers never initiate JSON-RPC requests. Elicitation replies carry an `action` of `accept`, `decline` or `cancel`, so a server must handle refusal, not assume approval.",
w:"A — cancelling discards work that may be most of the way done, and the whole purpose of `input_required` is that the decision can be made in flight. C — servers do not open channels or send their own requests; server-to-client interaction is embedded in results. D — retrying the original `tools/call` is the mechanism for a plain input-required *result* on a non-task call; once a task handle exists, updates go to the task via `tasks/update`."},

{id:2001,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which MCP task statuses are terminal — that is, once reached the task's state does not change again?",
o:["A. `working` and `completed`","B. `completed` only","C. `completed` and `failed`","D. `completed`, `failed`, and `cancelled`"],a:3,
e:"A task's lifecycle has five statuses. **`working`** means in progress and **`input_required`** means the server is waiting on the client — both are non-terminal. **`completed`**, **`failed`** and **`cancelled`** are terminal: `completed` carries the payload in `result`, `failed` carries a JSON-RPC error in `error`, and `cancelled` records that cancellation was honoured. A client polls `tasks/get` until it sees one of these three and then stops. Note that cancellation is cooperative — `tasks/cancel` records intent, and the task may still finish in `completed` or `failed` instead.",
w:"A — `working` is the ordinary in-progress state, the one you poll out of. B — omitting `failed` and `cancelled` would leave a client polling a dead task until its TTL expires. C — `cancelled` is equally terminal; a client that keeps polling after it wastes calls and can trip the server's rate limits."},

{id:2002,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A client receives a `CreateTaskResult` for a 25-minute export and polls `tasks/get` every 100 milliseconds. The server begins rejecting the polls with rate-limit errors, and by the time the client's backoff recovers the task's lifetime has elapsed and the payload is gone.\n\nWhat did the client get wrong?",
o:["A. It ignored the pacing the server supplied: the task handle carries both a suggested poll interval and a TTL, and the client should poll at the suggested rate and treat the TTL as its deadline for retrieving the outcome","B. `tasks/get` may be called only once per task, so the client should have waited for a notification","C. Tasks created from `tools/call` cannot be polled; only tasks created from `prompts/get` can","D. Polling is not permitted at all — the client was required to open a `subscriptions/listen` stream"],a:0,
e:"A `CreateTaskResult` returns a `taskId`, the initial status, a **TTL** and a **suggested polling interval**. Both are contract, not decoration: the interval is the server telling you how often it is willing to be asked, and the TTL is how long the handle and its result remain retrievable. Hammering the endpoint burns the client's own rate-limit budget and, as here, can starve it of the calls it needs before the handle expires. **Polling is the default** in this extension; `notifications/tasks` is an optional optimisation a client opts into.",
w:"B — `tasks/get` is designed to be called repeatedly; that is the whole polling loop. C — the extension applies to supported requests generally, including `tools/call`; nothing restricts it to prompts. D — notification delivery via `subscriptions/listen` is optional and only available if the server supports it; a client may rely on polling alone."},

{id:2003,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A hosted MCP server returns a `CreateTaskResult` for any tool call it expects to exceed ten seconds. Most consumers work. One third-party client renders the tool output as an unreadable object and never fetches a result; its requests never declared support for the tasks extension.\n\nWhich rule did the server break?",
o:["A. Tasks may only be returned from `prompts/get`, never from `tools/call`","B. The server must check the requesting message's declared capabilities and must never return a task to a client that did not opt into the tasks extension; without that opt-in it has to fall back to core behaviour","C. The server must emit a `notifications/tasks` message before its first `CreateTaskResult`","D. The client is at fault: handling a polymorphic result shape is mandatory for every MCP client regardless of capabilities"],a:1,
e:"Extensions are **opt-in and negotiated on both sides**. A client declares `io.modelcontextprotocol/tasks` in the `extensions` map of the capabilities it sends with each request; a server advertises the same identifier in its `server/discover` capabilities. The server-side implementation guidance is explicit: verify the client declared the extension **before** returning a `CreateTaskResult`, and never return a task to a client that did not. If one party supports an extension and the other does not, the supporting party must either fall back to core behaviour or reject the request with an appropriate error — silently emitting an unrecognised result shape is neither.",
w:"A — the extension augments supported requests generally, and `tools/call` is its primary case. C — `notifications/tasks` is an optional push channel a client subscribes to; it is not a precondition for returning a task, and sending notifications to a client that never opted in has the same defect as the original bug. D — a client is only obliged to handle result shapes for capabilities it declared; that is what per-request capability declaration is for."},

{id:2004,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An MCP server emits `notifications/progress` diligently through a three-minute export, incrementing a counter and including a human-readable message. The host displays no progress at all, and its console shows \"progress notification for unknown token\".\n\nWhat is wrong?",
o:["A. The server must first register the operation with `notifications/tasks`","B. Progress notifications are only delivered on stdio; the Streamable HTTP binding drops them","C. Progress tracking is client-initiated: the client must place a unique `progressToken` in the request's `_meta`, and the server may only emit notifications carrying a token from an active request","D. The server must set `total` on every notification or the client discards it"],a:2,
e:"Progress is **opt-in by the client**. When a client wants updates it includes a **`progressToken`** — a string or integer, unique across its active requests — in the originating request's `_meta`. The server then sends `notifications/progress` with `params` containing that same `progressToken` plus `progress` and optional `total` and `message`. Notifications **MUST** only reference tokens that came from an active request, which is exactly what the host's warning is reporting: the server invented a token, so there is nothing to attach the updates to. A server is always free to send none at all.",
w:"A — `notifications/tasks` belongs to the Tasks extension and pushes whole task states; it has no role in registering a progress token. B — both bindings carry request-scoped notifications: on Streamable HTTP they arrive on that request's SSE response stream before the final response, on stdio on the shared stdout channel. D — `total` is optional and may be omitted when unknown; `progress` alone is valid."},

{id:2005,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An MCP server exposes two operations. `validate_schema` takes about four seconds and moves through three internal phases the user would like to watch. `migrate_tenant` takes about 45 minutes and must survive the user closing the laptop lid and reopening the client later.\n\nWhich design is correct?",
o:["A. Both should use `notifications/progress`; the Tasks extension is only for human approval gates","B. Both should return a task, since progress notifications are deprecated","C. `validate_schema` should return a task, and `migrate_tenant` should block while emitting progress notifications","D. `validate_schema` should stay a normal call that emits `notifications/progress` on the open request; `migrate_tenant` should return a task the client polls with `tasks/get`"],a:3,
e:"The two mechanisms answer different questions. **`notifications/progress`** gives visibility *within* a request that is going to complete anyway — the notifications flow on that request's stream and die with it, so a four-second call with sub-phases is its ideal case. The **Tasks** extension gives a *durable handle* so the request need not stay open at all, which is what survives a suspended laptop, a dropped network or a client restart. The **decision rule**: if losing the connection would lose the work, you need a task; if you only need to narrate a call you are already holding, you need progress. They compose — a task can also report progress through its status messages.",
w:"A — progress cannot outlive its request, so a 45-minute operation would still be lost on disconnect; and Tasks covers long-running work generally, with human-in-the-loop being only one of its motivating cases. B — progress notifications are current, and returning a durable handle for a four-second call adds a polling round-trip for no benefit. C — this inverts the two, giving the short call a needless handle and leaving the long one exposed to exactly the timeout and restart failures tasks exist to prevent."},

{id:2006,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An MCP server processes files across a worker pool and emits a progress notification from each worker as it finishes, reporting that worker's own count. The client receives `progress` values of 30, 12, 55, 41, 78. One host's progress bar jumps backwards; another stops rendering updates entirely.\n\nWhich requirement is being violated?",
o:["A. For a given token, `progress` must increase with every notification even when `total` is unknown; the server should report from a single monotonic counter rather than per worker","B. `progress` must be an integer percentage between 0 and 100","C. `total` must be supplied before any `progress` value may be sent","D. Only one progress notification is permitted per request"],a:0,
e:"The spec requires that the **`progress` value MUST increase with each notification**, even if `total` is unknown. Both `progress` and `total` may be floating point, so there is no percentage constraint — the monotonicity is the constraint, because clients rely on it to render a bar without tracking history. The fix is structural rather than cosmetic: aggregate across the pool into one shared counter and emit from that, so the sequence is non-decreasing regardless of which worker finishes first. Both parties should also rate-limit to avoid flooding, and notifications must stop after completion.",
w:"B — floating-point values are explicitly permitted and there is no 0-to-100 rule; a server may count records or bytes. C — `total` is optional and may be omitted entirely when the size of the work is unknown. D — servers may send notifications at whatever frequency they judge appropriate, or none; frequency is not the defect here, ordering is."},

{id:2007,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An MCP `book_flight` tool is called with `departure_date` set to `2019-03-04`. The server currently responds with a JSON-RPC error, code `-32603`. Watching the transcript, the agent abandons the task and tells the user the booking system is unavailable — even though the only problem is a date in the past.\n\nWhat should the server return instead?",
o:["A. A JSON-RPC error with code `-32602`, which clients are required to forward to the model","B. An ordinary tool result with `isError: true` whose text names the problem and gives today's date, so the model can self-correct and retry","C. A `CreateTaskResult` with status `failed` and the validation message in `error`","D. An empty `content` array, letting the model infer that its arguments were rejected"],a:1,
e:"MCP separates two failure channels on purpose. **Protocol errors** — unknown tool, malformed request, server fault — are JSON-RPC errors for problems the model is unlikely to fix; clients MAY show them to the model, and recovery is unlikely. **Tool execution errors** — API failures, input validation, business rules — are returned as a normal result with **`isError: true`**, and clients SHOULD hand those to the model precisely so it can self-correct. A past date is textbook validation feedback. The **transferable rule**: if the model could plausibly fix it by retrying differently, make it actionable content, not a protocol fault.",
w:"A — swapping `-32603` for `-32602` fixes the code but keeps the failure in the channel clients are told is unlikely to produce recovery; the model is only *permitted* to see it, not guaranteed to. C — a task handle is for long-running work; wrapping an instantaneous validation failure in one adds a polling round-trip and still hides the guidance in an error field. D — an empty result is indistinguishable from a genuine no-match and gives the model nothing to correct."},

{id:2008,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A client sends `tools/call` with a `name` the server does not implement. How should the server report it?",
o:["A. A tool result with `isError: true`, so the model can choose a different tool","B. A tool result with an empty `content` array and `isError` left unset","C. A JSON-RPC error response, because an unknown tool is a defect in the request itself rather than actionable feedback the model can act on","D. A `CreateTaskResult` whose status is `cancelled`"],a:2,
e:"The spec assigns **unknown tool** to the *protocol error* category, alongside malformed requests and server faults — these are returned as standard JSON-RPC errors (the example uses `-32602`). The reasoning is about recovery odds: the model has no way to invent a tool the server does not expose, so the failure is a client or wiring bug, not something to reason about. Contrast this with tool *execution* errors — upstream failures, bad argument values, business-rule violations — which go back as a result with `isError: true` so the model can adjust and retry. Clients SHOULD forward the latter to the model and MAY forward the former.",
w:"A — plausible, and this is the correct channel for a bad argument *value*, but a name that does not exist cannot be repaired by the model and the spec classes it as a protocol error. B — an empty successful result claims the call worked and found nothing, which is a different and false statement. D — task statuses describe the lifecycle of work that was actually accepted; there is no work here, and returning a handle to a client that may not support the extension compounds the error."},

{id:2009,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"Every tool on an internal MCP server reports failure the same way: a result with `isError: true` and the text \"operation failed\". The consuming agent therefore applies one policy — retry up to five times, then give up. Two common failures are an upstream 429 and a postcode the upstream validator rejects.\n\nWhat does replacing that string with a structured error envelope actually change?",
o:["A. Nothing at the agent level: retry policy belongs entirely to the client and cannot be informed by the server","B. It lets the server retry internally, removing the need for the agent to retry at all","C. It converts every failure into a JSON-RPC error, which clients do not retry","D. Carrying a category, a retryability flag and suggested alternatives lets the agent back off and retry the throttled call while immediately re-prompting for a corrected postcode instead of burning five attempts on a value that will never succeed"],a:3,
e:"A **structured error envelope** turns an opaque string into a decision the caller can make deterministically. The three fields that matter are the **category** (is this a throttle, a validation failure, an authorisation problem, an upstream outage?), **retryability** (is another attempt capable of succeeding, and after how long?) and **alternatives** (a narrower query, a different tool, a corrected argument). Retry is still the client's decision — but it is now an *informed* one, which is the point. The **transferable principle**: the server owns the classification because only it knows what failed; the client owns the policy.",
w:"A — the policy is indeed the client's, but the classification is not, and a client cannot classify what the server described only as \"operation failed\"; five blind retries on a bad postcode is the direct cost. B — internal retries help for transient faults but cannot fix an invalid argument, and hiding all retries behind a blocking call is how you get proxy timeouts. C — the opposite of what is wanted: recoverable failures belong in results with `isError: true` so the model sees them, while protocol errors are the channel clients are least likely to recover from."},

{id:2010,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An MCP server's `get_account_balance` tool receives a 503 from the upstream ledger. Rather than fail, it returns `{ \"balance\": 0.00, \"currency\": \"USD\" }` with `isError` unset, on the reasoning that \"the agent shouldn't crash on a blip.\"\n\nAssess this choice.",
o:["A. It is the worst of the available options: a fabricated success is indistinguishable from a real answer, so the agent states a false balance with full confidence. The server must surface the failure as an error result carrying its category, its retryability and any alternative","B. Acceptable, provided the server logs the upstream failure on its own side","C. Acceptable, because `isError` is optional and defaults to `false`","D. Better than an opaque failure, because the agent can always treat a zero balance as a sentinel"],a:0,
e:"There is a ranking here worth internalising. A **structured error** is best: the agent can back off, retry, or tell the user the ledger is unreachable. An **opaque failure** is bad — the agent knows something broke but not what, so it retries blindly or gives up. A **fabricated success** is worst, because it destroys the agent's ability to know anything is wrong; downstream reasoning, user-facing statements and any tool chained onto the value all inherit the fiction silently. The **transferable rule**: a tool may fail, but it must never lie. Honest failure preserves the agent's capacity to recover; invented data removes it.",
w:"B — server-side logs help the operator after the fact and do nothing for the agent or the user in the moment, who are the ones acting on the wrong number. C — `isError` defaulting to false is precisely why omitting it asserts success; the default is not a licence to misreport. D — zero is a perfectly legal balance, so it is not a sentinel; the agent cannot distinguish an empty account from an unreachable ledger."},

{id:2011,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An MCP server exposes a project directory through `file://` resources. It resolves `resources/read` by concatenating its configured base directory with the path from the requested URI. A test with the URI `file:///../../../etc/shadow` returns the file's contents.\n\nWhat is the correct fix?",
o:["A. The client should have rejected the URI, since URI validation is a client-side responsibility","B. The server must treat the URI as untrusted input — canonicalise the resolved path, reject anything that escapes the configured base directory, and authorise the read independently of what was asked for","C. Add a line to the tool and resource descriptions telling the model not to request paths outside the project","D. Move to Streamable HTTP, since HTTP URLs cannot contain `..` segments"],a:1,
e:"The requested `uri` arrives from a model that may have read untrusted content, so it is **untrusted input** regardless of how it looks. The spec is direct: servers **MUST** validate all resource URIs and **MUST** sanitise file paths to prevent directory traversal when serving `file://` resources. Correct handling is to resolve to an absolute canonical path and verify it is still inside the permitted root — string filtering for `..` is defeated by encodings and symlinks. Note the related point about roots: they are informational guidance, not an access-control mechanism, and the protocol does not enforce that a server stays inside them. **Principle: the model is not a security boundary; enforce in code, on the server.**",
w:"A — clients are told to validate root URIs too, but defence has to hold on the side that touches the filesystem; a server that trusts its caller is exploitable by any caller. C — a description is advisory text the model may ignore or be manipulated past by injected content; it is guidance, never enforcement. D — the transport is irrelevant to path resolution, and traversal sequences survive URL encoding perfectly well."},

{id:2012,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An internal MCP server exposes a `run_query` tool over the analytics warehouse. It connects using a database account that holds owner-level rights on the schema. The server's instructions and the tool description both state that only read-only queries are permitted.\n\nAssess the design.",
o:["A. Adequate, because `tools/list` advertises the tool as read-only through its annotations","B. Adequate, because the host requires user confirmation before every tool invocation","C. Inadequate. The instruction is advisory and the model is not a security boundary, so the server's own database connection must use a least-privilege read-only role — then a mis-generated or injected statement physically cannot write","D. Inadequate, and the fix is a regular expression that rejects statements beginning with `DROP` or `DELETE`"],a:2,
e:"**Least privilege bounds blast radius.** Everything above the connection — the description, the server instructions, the model's intent — is advisory; the only control that holds under a prompt-injected or simply mistaken statement is the privilege the connection actually has. Grant a read-only role and a destructive statement fails in the database, deterministically, with no reliance on the model behaving. The spec puts the same duty on servers directly: validate all tool inputs, implement proper access controls, rate limit invocations. **Transferable principle: the model proposes, deterministic code authorises.**",
w:"A — annotations are server-supplied hints that clients **MUST** treat as untrusted unless the server is trusted; they describe behaviour, they do not constrain it. B — confirmation is a valuable human-in-the-loop control but the reviewer is approving natural-language intent, not auditing generated SQL, and approval fatigue is real. D — keyword filtering is a blocklist over an expressive language: comments, casing, CTEs, stored procedures and `UPDATE` all slip past. Useful as defence in depth, worthless as the boundary."},

{id:2013,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A platform team wraps a 340-operation internal API as an MCP server, generating one tool per operation so that \"any future client has everything.\" Two months in: tool selection is unreliable across all consumers, every consuming client pays the token cost of the definitions on every request, and a security review notes that a read-only reporting assistant can reach `terminate_cluster`.\n\nWhat is the right correction?",
o:["A. Keep all 340 and instruct each consuming client to denylist the dangerous ones","B. Keep all 340 but cut every description to a single line to reduce token cost","C. Split into 340 separate MCP servers, one per operation","D. Curate: expose the smaller set of task-shaped operations consumers actually need, with descriptions good enough to disambiguate them, and keep destructive operations out of the server entirely unless a consumer genuinely requires them"],a:3,
e:"A server's surface is a **least-privilege decision**, not a completeness exercise. Mechanically transcribing an API produces near-duplicate names the model cannot choose between, definitions that are billed as input tokens on every request from every consumer, and reachable destructive capability that no amount of prompting reliably fences off. The remedy runs in the other direction: expose operations shaped like the tasks consumers actually perform, invest in descriptions, and let capability absence — not instruction — enforce what an assistant cannot do. Where a large surface is genuinely needed, deferred loading with a tool-search mechanism keeps definitions out of context, but it does not repair bad decomposition.",
w:"A — pushing safety onto each consumer means every current and future client must remember the same denylist, and one that forgets inherits full destructive reach; a server-side omission cannot be forgotten. B — shorter descriptions worsen the selection problem the team already has, and definitions still ship on every request, so it trades reliability for a small token saving. C — 340 servers multiplies process, transport, auth and version-compatibility overhead by 340 and makes cross-operation naming collisions worse, not better."},

{id:2014,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"In review, an engineer objects to `\"description\": \"Runs a report.\"` on a tool inside a shared MCP server, saying that a hand-written client-side tool might get away with it but a server cannot.\n\nWhat is the substance of that objection?",
o:["A. The server's description is the one interface contract every consuming client inherits, and a client author cannot correct it without forking the server — so a vague description degrades selection accuracy in every consumer simultaneously","B. MCP validates descriptions against a minimum length and rejects short ones at `tools/list`","C. The description is what the client uses to derive the tool's `outputSchema`","D. Descriptions inside a server are billed at a different rate from client-side tool descriptions"],a:0,
e:"This is the flip side of MCP's reuse value. The reason to build a server is that you **write the integration once and every MCP-capable client consumes it** — which means the quality of `name`, `description` and `inputSchema` is multiplied by the number of consumers rather than confined to one codebase. A client can disable a tool or defer its loading, but it cannot rewrite the description it was handed. A good one states what the tool does, when to prefer it over its neighbours, what the arguments mean and what comes back — the same discipline as any tool definition, with the stakes multiplied.",
w:"B — the protocol constrains tool *names* (character set, length guidance, server-scoped uniqueness) but does not police description quality; nothing rejects \"Runs a report.\" C — `outputSchema` is an optional JSON Schema the server author writes to describe `structuredContent`; it is never inferred from prose. D — tool definitions are billed as input tokens wherever they come from; the difference is how many consumers pay for a bad one, not the rate."},

{id:2015,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A host integrates a community MCP server. One tool, `sync_repository`, carries an annotation asserting it is read-only, so the host suppresses its usual confirmation prompt for that tool. In use, the tool force-pushes and destroys uncommitted work.\n\nWhich conclusion should the host team draw about their own design?",
o:["A. The server violated a normative requirement, so the host's logic was sound and the server should be reported","B. Annotations are hints supplied by the server and must be treated as untrusted unless the server itself is trusted, so they can never be the sole basis for suppressing a confirmation","C. The host should have re-read `tools/list` immediately before the call to refresh the annotation","D. Annotations apply only to resources, so the host misread the schema"],a:1,
e:"The specification carries an explicit warning: clients **MUST** consider tool annotations to be untrusted unless they come from a trusted server. Descriptions and annotations are self-reported claims about behaviour, not enforced constraints — the same category as `serverInfo`, which the spec also says clients SHOULD NOT rely on for security decisions. A host may use annotations to *improve* a UI (ordering, iconography, wording) but the gate on a destructive action has to rest on something it controls: its own trust policy for the server, its own allow/denylist, or a confirmation it does not let the server waive. **The model is not a security boundary, and neither is a server's self-description.**",
w:"A — the server's claim was indeed wrong, but a host whose safety gate can be disabled by a field the server sends has no gate; blame does not restore the user's work. C — re-fetching gives you a fresher copy of the same untrusted assertion. D — annotations are shared across tools, prompts, resources and content blocks; tool annotations specifically are what the warning addresses."},

{id:2016,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A developer is iterating on a new stdio MCP server entirely through a desktop client: edit code, fully quit and reopen the client, retry in chat, then read the client's log files. Tool failures surface only as the assistant declining to answer, and each cycle takes several minutes.\n\nWhat is the better development loop?",
o:["A. Point the Messages API MCP connector at the server, since that gives the fastest feedback for a local build","B. Write a bespoke JSON-RPC harness, because no general-purpose MCP tooling exists","C. Drive the server directly with the MCP Inspector — a transport-agnostic testing UI that connects to stdio or Streamable HTTP servers, lists and invokes tools, prompts and resources, and shows the raw notification stream — and only wire it into a client once it behaves","D. Use the client's Chrome DevTools network panel, which shows the stdio frames"],a:2,
e:"The documented workflow is **Inspector first, client second**. The Inspector removes the model and the host UI from the loop, so you see the actual JSON-RPC exchange: what `tools/list` returned, what arguments arrived, what came back, which notifications fired. Pair it with **`stderr`** logging — captured by the host on stdio, though *not* captured by the client under Streamable HTTP, where you need your own aggregation or OpenTelemetry. Note that protocol-level logging via `notifications/message` is deprecated as of the current revision. Only once the server is correct in isolation is a client the right place to test integration behaviour.",
w:"A — the Messages API MCP connector requires a publicly reachable HTTPS server and explicitly cannot connect to a local stdio server, so it cannot be used for this loop at all. B — the Inspector exists precisely for this and is recommended as the first stop; a bespoke harness is wasted effort. D — stdio frames are pipes between processes, not network traffic; DevTools shows nothing, and it is a client-side tool for client-side errors."},

{id:2017,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A stdio MCP server starts and runs correctly when launched from the developer's terminal. Launched by the desktop client from the same machine, it exits immediately, and the client's log shows a failure opening `./config/settings.yaml`.\n\nWhat is the most likely cause?",
o:["A. The client strips the server's environment, so the configuration file cannot be located","B. The server must be rewritten to fetch its configuration over Streamable HTTP","C. The client launches servers with a read-only filesystem","D. The working directory of a client-launched server is undefined, so relative paths resolve somewhere unexpected; both the launch configuration and the server's own file references must use absolute paths"],a:3,
e:"When a client launches a stdio server it is a **subprocess of the host**, and the host may itself have been started from anywhere — the documented guidance is that the working directory may be undefined (for example `/` on macOS). Run the same binary from your terminal and the working directory is your project, so relative paths happen to resolve; run it from the client and they do not. Use absolute paths in the launch configuration, in `.env` references and in the server's own file access. A closely related trap in the same area: a stdio server inherits only a limited, platform-dependent subset of environment variables, so anything it needs should be passed explicitly through the configuration's `env` block.",
w:"A — the environment is narrowed, not stripped, and the error names a *path* resolution failure rather than a missing variable; the `env` block is the fix for the variable case. B — configuration loading has nothing to do with the transport, and moving to HTTP would not change how a relative path resolves. C — no such restriction is imposed by the transport; the server ran fine from a terminal on the same filesystem."},

{id:2018,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A shared MCP server renames `run_report`'s required argument from `range` to `date_range` and deploys. Three consuming applications break: two hold prompt-cached tool blocks from earlier in their conversations, and one has long-running agent sessions that keep sending `range`, which now fails schema validation.\n\nWhat should the team have done, and what should they do now?",
o:["A. Make the change additive: keep accepting `range` as a deprecated alias alongside the new `date_range`, require neither at the schema level while validating in code that exactly one is present, and remove `range` only after consumers have migrated","B. Bump the server's version in `serverInfo`, which clients use to invalidate cached tool definitions","C. Emit `notifications/tools/list_changed` before deploying, which forces every client to re-read the schema atomically","D. Declare a new protocol version in `server/discover` so that clients on the old interface are rejected cleanly"],a:0,
e:"A server's tool surface is a **published interface**, and consumers hold copies of it — in prompt caches, in running agent sessions, in generated client code. Breaking changes must therefore be **additive first, subtractive later**: add the new argument, keep the old one working, tolerate both for a deprecation window, then remove. Note the interaction with caching specifically: a cacheable prefix must be byte-identical, so changing a tool definition invalidates every consumer's cached prefix at the moment it changes — another reason to change once, deliberately, rather than incrementally.",
w:"B — `serverInfo` is self-reported, is not verified by the protocol, and the spec says clients SHOULD NOT change their behaviour based on it; nothing invalidates a cache on the strength of it. C — `list_changed` only reaches clients holding an open `subscriptions/listen` stream with the relevant filter, it is a SHOULD rather than a guarantee, and it cannot retroactively fix a session already mid-flight. D — the protocol version is the MCP wire revision, shared across the ecosystem; it is not a slot for your server's interface version, and misusing it locks out conforming clients."},

{id:2019,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An MCP knowledge-base server reorganises its addressing from `wiki://page/onboarding` to `wiki://v2/page/onboarding` and deletes the old URIs in the same release. Users' saved contexts and stored agent state now fail with `-32602`, and one host's embedded `resource_link` references all dangle.\n\nWhat should the team have done?",
o:["A. Nothing — resource URIs are internal identifiers and clients are obliged to re-list before every read","B. Treated the URI as the durable public identifier consumers store: kept the old URIs resolvable to the same content during a deprecation window, published the new scheme alongside, and retired the old one only afterwards","C. Declared the `resources.subscribe` capability so clients were notified of the change","D. Returned the old URIs with an empty `contents` array so clients could detect the retirement"],a:1,
e:"A resource **URI is the durable handle**, and the protocol assumes consumers keep it: hosts save it in user contexts, tools return it as a `resource_link`, and stored agent state carries it across sessions. Retiring one is therefore the same class of change as removing a tool argument — additive first, subtractive later. Keep the old URI resolving (serving the same content, or mapping internally to the new location), advertise the new scheme in `resources/list` and any templates, and drop the old one once consumers have migrated. **Principle: anything a consumer can persist is public interface, and public interface gets a deprecation window.**",
w:"A — re-listing does not help, because a saved reference is a URI rather than a position in a list, and `resource_link` values returned by tools are explicitly not guaranteed to appear in `resources/list` at all. C — `subscribe` delivers `notifications/resources/updated` for URIs a client is already watching; it cannot notify anyone about a URI that no longer exists. D — an empty `contents` array for a non-existent resource is exactly what the spec forbids, because it is ambiguous between empty and absent."},

{id:2020,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"In the current MCP specification revision, how do a client and a server establish which protocol version and which optional capabilities are in play?",
o:["A. A one-time `initialize` request opens a session, and the capabilities negotiated there hold for the lifetime of the connection","B. Capabilities are inferred from the transport: stdio implies the full feature set and Streamable HTTP a reduced one","C. Every request carries its protocol version and the client's capabilities in `_meta`, and the server accepts or rejects each request independently; `server/discover` is available for reading a server's supported versions and capabilities up front","D. The client sends a `capabilities/negotiate` request before any other method"],a:2,
e:"**Currency note — this changed.** The base protocol is now **stateless with per-request metadata**: there is no negotiation handshake, and each request declares `io.modelcontextprotocol/protocolVersion` and `io.modelcontextprotocol/clientCapabilities` in `_meta` (mirrored into the `MCP-Protocol-Version` header on Streamable HTTP). A server that does not implement the requested version answers `UnsupportedProtocolVersionError` listing what it does support. Servers **MUST** implement `server/discover`, which returns `supportedVersions`, `capabilities` and `serverInfo`; calling it is optional for clients. Extensions such as Tasks are advertised in an `extensions` map inside capabilities.",
w:"A — this is the *legacy* model, used by revisions up to `2025-11-25`; dual-era implementations still support it for backward compatibility, but it is no longer how the current revision works. B — protocol semantics are identical on every transport; a binding defines only framing and metadata carriage, never the feature set. D — no such method exists; discovery is `server/discover` and it reports rather than negotiates."},

{id:2021,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A finance MCP server that each analyst previously ran locally over stdio is moved to a single HTTPS endpoint serving 400 people. It keeps the one service account it always used, which can read every cost centre. The audit log now attributes every query to that account.\n\nAssess the deployment.",
o:["A. Acceptable, because hosting does not change MCP's message format","B. Acceptable, provided the endpoint is reachable only from inside the corporate network","C. The remedy is for each client to pass the analyst's name as a tool argument that the server records","D. The server must authenticate each caller and authorise against that verified identity — scoping results to the caller's own cost centres and attributing every call to them — because an identity that the model or the client merely asserts is not a credential"],a:3,
e:"Running locally, the operating system did the authorisation for you: the subprocess had exactly the user's own access, and there was one user. Centrally hosted, that coupling is gone and the server becomes a **confused deputy** — it holds broad privilege and acts on requests from many principals. What must be built: per-user authentication (on HTTP transports, OAuth 2.1 bearer tokens the server validates, including checking that the token's **audience** is itself), authorisation derived from the verified identity, tenant isolation of any stored state, rate limiting, and audit records tied to the real caller. **Authorise against the authenticated session, never an identity the model or client asserts.**",
w:"A — true and irrelevant: the wire format is unchanged, which is exactly why the work is in authentication, authorisation, isolation and auditing rather than in the handlers. B — network placement is one control among several; it does nothing about a legitimate analyst reading another cost centre or about an audit trail that cannot name anyone. C — a client-supplied name is unverified input that can be forged; the spec is explicit that servers MUST NOT rely on client-provided user identification, and should derive identity from the token instead."},

{id:2022,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A hosted MCP server wraps a third-party CRM. To avoid storing credentials, it takes the OAuth bearer token the MCP client sent on the request and forwards it verbatim to the CRM API.\n\nWhy is this pattern prohibited?",
o:["A. It is token passthrough. A server must validate that a token was issued specifically for it — the audience — and must not accept or forward tokens issued for other resources; it should hold its own CRM credential, bound to the verified user, instead","B. It is acceptable as long as the token is only ever transmitted over TLS","C. It is prohibited only because bearer tokens expire faster than MCP sessions do","D. It is the recommended pattern, since it lets a server avoid storing third-party credentials"],a:0,
e:"**Token passthrough** is named and forbidden. Two defects compound: failing to validate the audience means the server accepts tokens minted for other services, breaking a core OAuth boundary; forwarding them unmodified makes the server a confused deputy, because the downstream API trusts a token it assumes someone validated. The normative rules are blunt — an MCP server **MUST** validate that access tokens were issued specifically for it, **MUST** only accept tokens valid for its own resources, and **MUST NOT** accept or transit any other tokens. The sanctioned way for a server to reach a third party on a user's behalf is to obtain its own credential — typically by acting as an OAuth client through URL-mode elicitation — and bind the resulting tokens to the verified user identity.",
w:"B — transport encryption protects a token in flight and says nothing about whether the recipient was its intended audience; the vulnerability is trust, not interception. C — lifetime is an operational nuisance; the prohibition rests on audience validation, circumvented security controls and broken audit trails. D — inverted: avoiding credential storage is the stated *motivation* for the anti-pattern, and the spec requires the server to be stateful enough to manage its own third-party tokens instead."},

{id:2023,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"Because MCP has no protocol-level session, a hosted server threads a multi-step approval workflow through an explicit handle: `create_approval` returns a `workflow_id`, and later tools accept it as an ordinary argument. During testing an engineer passes a colleague's `workflow_id` and reads and modifies their record.\n\nWhat is the defect and the fix?",
o:["A. The handle should have been returned in `structuredContent` rather than in a `content` text block","B. Possession of a handle is not authentication. The server must bind each handle to the authenticated subject — keying stored state by the verified user id and rejecting a handle presented by any other principal — and use unguessable, expiring identifiers","C. The workflow should be moved into a protocol-level session, which cannot be forged","D. The tool description should state that workflow handles must not be shared between users"],a:1,
e:"This is **state handle hijacking**. Because the protocol is stateless, a handle travels as a plain string in a tool result and returns as a plain argument — so it is model-supplied, client-supplied, **untrusted input** on every subsequent call. The normative requirements: servers that implement authorization **MUST** verify all inbound requests and **MUST NOT** treat possession of a handle as authentication. Practically, key stored state as verified-user-id plus handle, where the user id comes from the validated token rather than the request body, and reject any mismatch. Handles should additionally be non-deterministic, generated with a secure random source, and bounded in lifetime.",
w:"A — where the handle appears in the result is a formatting choice; `structuredContent` is server-produced data with no access-control meaning. C — the current revision deliberately has no protocol-level session, and the sessions of earlier revisions were themselves subject to hijacking; reintroducing one would not authorise anything. D — a description is advisory prose the model may ignore, and here the attacker is a caller deliberately supplying someone else's handle."},

{id:2024,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An MCP server is lifted unchanged from a per-user local stdio deployment onto a shared internal endpoint. One team's agent enters a retry loop against `search_tickets`, and within minutes the upstream ticketing API begins throttling requests from every other team as well.\n\nWhich change addresses the class of problem?",
o:["A. Add a loop guard to the offending agent; the server has no role here","B. Revert to stdio so that every user runs their own process again","C. A centrally hosted server is a shared resource and needs per-caller rate limiting and quotas, so one caller's runaway loop is throttled against its own budget instead of consuming the shared upstream allowance — a concern that never arose when each user ran their own subprocess","D. Increase the upstream API plan so the shared allowance cannot be exhausted"],a:2,
e:"Local stdio gave you isolation for free: one subprocess per user, each with its own quota, and a misbehaving agent could only harm its owner. Central hosting collapses that into a shared fate, so **rate limiting becomes a server responsibility** — the spec lists rate-limiting tool invocations among the things servers MUST do. The control must be **per authenticated caller**, not global, or the throttle simply relocates the collateral damage. This sits alongside the other things that change on hosting: per-user authentication and authorisation, tenant isolation of stored state, network exposure, and audit logging — while the JSON-RPC message format stays identical.",
w:"A — client-side loop guards are worth having but they are the fix for one agent, not for the shared resource; the next consumer can reproduce the outage. B — reverting solves it by abandoning the reason for hosting, and is not available when the server needs central credentials or private network access. D — a bigger allowance moves the threshold without changing the dynamic; a runaway loop will find any ceiling, and every other tenant still pays for it."},

{id:2025,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team is scoping the work to take a functioning stdio MCP server and host it centrally for the whole organisation. Which statement about that migration is accurate?",
o:["A. The primitives change: a hosted server may expose only tools, not resources or prompts","B. The JSON-RPC methods are renamed to HTTP equivalents, so every handler must be rewritten","C. Tool arguments must be flattened to strings, because JSON-RPC cannot be carried over HTTP","D. The methods and message shapes are unchanged; the new work sits around the server — per-user authentication and authorisation, tenant isolation, network exposure, rate limiting and audit logging — plus swapping stdio framing for the Streamable HTTP binding"],a:3,
e:"**Protocol semantics are identical on every transport.** A binding defines only how messages are framed and delivered and how cancellation and metadata are carried; `tools/call`, `resources/read` and `prompts/get` mean exactly the same thing on either. So the handler logic largely survives, and the estimate should sit almost entirely in the operational envelope: authenticating each caller (OAuth 2.1 bearer tokens with audience validation on HTTP), authorising against that verified identity, isolating any stored state per tenant, exposing and hardening a network endpoint, rate limiting per caller, and auditing. Plus the concrete binding work — POST per message, the `Accept` and `MCP-Protocol-Version` headers, `Origin` validation.",
w:"A — all three primitives are transport-independent; note separately that Claude's Messages API MCP connector currently surfaces only tool calls, but that is a client-side limitation, not a property of hosted servers. B — the method names are part of the core protocol, not the binding; they are unchanged, and on Streamable HTTP the method is merely mirrored into an `Mcp-Method` header. C — the body of every POST is a JSON-RPC message with structured `params`; nothing is flattened."},

{id:2026,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"In the current Streamable HTTP binding, how does a client send a JSON-RPC request, and how may the server reply?",
o:["A. Each message is its own HTTP POST to a single MCP endpoint; the server answers with either one `application/json` object or a `text/event-stream` SSE stream scoped to that request, and the client must support both","B. The client opens one long-lived HTTP GET SSE stream for all server output and POSTs its requests to a second endpoint delivered in an `endpoint` event","C. The client opens a WebSocket to the MCP endpoint and multiplexes all requests over it","D. Each message is an HTTP PUT, and an SSE response is mandatory for every request"],a:0,
e:"**Streamable HTTP** exposes a single MCP endpoint that accepts **POST**. Every JSON-RPC request or notification is its own POST; the client **MUST** send an `Accept` header listing both `application/json` and `text/event-stream`, plus an `MCP-Protocol-Version` header matching the version in the body's `_meta`. The server chooses per request whether to answer with a single JSON object or an SSE stream carrying request-scoped notifications such as `notifications/progress` before the final response. **Currency note:** the current revision removed the standalone GET stream, protocol-level sessions via `Mcp-Session-Id`, and `Last-Event-ID` resumability — a modern server answers GET or DELETE on the endpoint with `405`.",
w:"B — that describes the deprecated HTTP+SSE transport from `2024-11-05`, whose opening GET returned an `endpoint` event; it survives only as a compatibility fallback. C — MCP defines no WebSocket binding; the standard bindings are stdio and Streamable HTTP, with custom transports permitted but required to preserve the message format and patterns. D — the method is POST, and SSE is one of two permitted response forms rather than a requirement."},

{id:2027,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"A 2024 tutorial instructs you to implement the HTTP+SSE transport for a new remote MCP server. What is the current guidance?",
o:["A. HTTP+SSE remains the recommended remote transport, and Streamable HTTP is an optional optimisation on top of it","B. Streamable HTTP replaced HTTP+SSE, which is deprecated; new implementations should adopt Streamable HTTP, and only compatibility shims should still speak the old transport","C. Both were withdrawn, and remote servers now use stdio over an SSH tunnel","D. HTTP+SSE and Streamable HTTP are two names for the same transport"],a:1,
e:"**Streamable HTTP was introduced as a replacement for the HTTP+SSE transport** of protocol version `2024-11-05`, and HTTP+SSE has been deprecated ever since. New implementations SHOULD NOT adopt it; existing ones SHOULD migrate. The two standard bindings today are **stdio** (client-launched subprocess over stdin and stdout) and **Streamable HTTP** (POST per message to one endpoint, answered as JSON or a request-scoped SSE stream). Backward compatibility with the old transport is possible — a client can fall back to the opening GET after a POST fails without a recognisable modern error — but that is a shim, not a design target.",
w:"A — inverted: HTTP+SSE is the deprecated predecessor, not the recommendation. C — both HTTP bindings exist; stdio is for local subprocesses and defines no remote tunnelling story. D — they are genuinely different: HTTP+SSE used a persistent GET stream plus a separate POST endpoint discovered from an `endpoint` event, whereas Streamable HTTP uses a single endpoint with one POST per message."},

{id:2028,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team is deploying two MCP servers. The first reads and edits files in an individual developer's local checkout. The second is a shared deployment-status service used by 200 engineers and must sit behind the company's single sign-on.\n\nWhich transport suits each, and why?",
o:["A. stdio for both, tunnelling the shared service to each engineer's machine","B. Streamable HTTP for both, so one deployment story covers them","C. stdio for the local file server — the client launches it as a subprocess, it inherits the user's own filesystem access and exposes no network surface — and Streamable HTTP for the shared service, which has to be reachable over the network and authenticate each engineer individually","D. Streamable HTTP for the file server, because filesystem access needs a URL scheme, and stdio for the shared service, because it has lower latency"],a:2,
e:"The choice follows from **where the work and the trust live**. A local file server wants **stdio**: the client starts it as a subprocess, its privileges are already the user's, its lifecycle is tied to the client, and there is no listening port to secure — the security guidance even recommends stdio specifically to limit access to just the MCP client. A shared service wants **Streamable HTTP**: it must be reachable, must authenticate and authorise each caller, and must rate limit and audit per user. The messages are identical either way; only the framing and the surrounding operational obligations differ.",
w:"A — tunnelling a per-user subprocess to 200 people gives every engineer a separate process with the server's credentials and no central authorisation point; stdio has no multi-user story. B — running an HTTP listener on every developer's laptop for local file access adds a network surface for no benefit, which is why the spec tells local HTTP servers to bind to loopback and require a token. D — `file://` URIs are resource identifiers and imply nothing about transport, and stdio is unavailable for a remote shared service regardless of latency."},

{id:2029,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"A client abandons an in-flight MCP request. How is that cancellation signalled?",
o:["A. Identically on both standard transports: the client sends a `notifications/cancelled` notification referencing the request id","B. Identically on both standard transports: the client sends `tasks/cancel` referencing the request id","C. On stdio the client closes and relaunches the subprocess; on Streamable HTTP it sends `notifications/cancelled`","D. On stdio the client sends `notifications/cancelled` referencing the request id; on Streamable HTTP it closes that request's response stream, which the server must treat as cancellation"],a:3,
e:"Cancellation is one of the few places where the **binding** genuinely differs while the protocol-level rules stay the same. On **stdio** every message shares one bidirectional channel, so there is no per-request stream to close and the client **MUST** send `notifications/cancelled` naming the request id. On **Streamable HTTP** each request has its own response stream, so closing it is an unambiguous transport-level signal the server **MUST** treat as cancellation — the current revision defines no client-to-server notifications on that binding at all. Either way the server SHOULD stop work promptly and **MUST NOT** send further messages for that request.",
w:"A — correct for stdio only; on Streamable HTTP no `notifications/cancelled` message is expected, because closing the stream carries the signal. B — `tasks/cancel` is a different mechanism from the Tasks extension: it targets a durable `taskId` rather than an in-flight request id, and it is cooperative, so the task may still end `completed` or `failed`. C — killing the subprocess would abandon every other in-flight request on that connection; the spec provides a per-request notification precisely to avoid that."},

{id:2030,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An internal team built an MCP server to expose their pricing engine to a single consumer: their own agent, in a codebase they also own. A year on, no second consumer has appeared, and the team maintains a separate service, its transport handling, its own authentication, schema plumbing, a release process and a compatibility matrix against the agent's versions.\n\nWhat did they misjudge?",
o:["A. They paid MCP's real costs — a separate process or service, a transport, its own authentication and lifecycle, and an interface contract that must stay backward-compatible — without collecting its payoff, which is reuse by additional MCP-capable clients. With one known consumer, client-side tool definitions inside the agent were the proportionate choice","B. They should have chosen MCP but used stdio, which removes the overhead entirely","C. The mistake is architectural: an agent should never call tools that are defined in its own codebase","D. The overhead is the same either way, so the decision made no practical difference"],a:0,
e:"MCP's value proposition is specific and worth stating honestly: **write the integration once and consume it from any MCP-capable client** — a coding agent, a desktop app, a custom orchestrator — instead of reimplementing it per consumer. That reuse is what repays the overhead of a discoverable, versioned, separately deployed interface. With a **single known consumer inside your own codebase**, there is no reuse to collect, and a plain client-side tool definition is simpler and easier to change. The **decision rule**: count the distinct consumers you actually expect, then judge whether the interface work is repaid. Choose MCP when it is; do not choose it as a default.",
w:"B — stdio removes the network and hosting layer but not the process boundary, the JSON-RPC surface, the schema plumbing or the version contract, which is where most of the cost sits. C — calling in-process tools is entirely normal and is how most agents work; MCP is for crossing a boundary, not a requirement for tool use. D — the costs are asymmetric and that is the whole point: a client-side tool is a function and a schema, while a server is a deployable artefact with a public contract."},

{id:2031,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"The MCP specification describes prompts as **user-controlled**. What does that mean?",
o:["A. The user, rather than the server, authors the prompt's content","B. Prompts are exposed so that the user can explicitly select them — typically as slash commands or a picker — rather than being invoked autonomously by the model; the server still authors the content","C. The user must approve each prompt's text before the server is permitted to return it","D. Prompts can only be listed for users that the server has authenticated"],a:1,
e:"The specification spells the distinction out: prompts are user-controlled, and that **refers to who decides when the prompt is used, not who authors its content** — prompt content is defined by the server. The canonical surfacing is a slash command, which is why prompts fit named, repeatable workflows a user chooses to start. Contrast the other two primitives: **tools are model-controlled**, discovered and invoked by the model, and **resources are application-driven**, with the host deciding how to bring content into context. The practical consequence is that a capability the model must be able to reach on its own initiative belongs in a tool, not a prompt.",
w:"A — the server authors prompt content and returns it from `prompts/get`; the user chooses which one to invoke and supplies its arguments. C — there is no per-return approval step; the user's control is exercised by selecting the prompt in the first place. D — visibility may legitimately vary with the authorization presented on a request, but that is an access-control property of any primitive, not what user-controlled means."},

{id:2032,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What does a successful `resources/read` return for a single resource?",
o:["A. A `content` array of text blocks only; binary resources have to be exposed as tools instead","B. A bare base64 string with no metadata, leaving the client to infer the type from the URI's extension","C. A `contents` array whose entries each carry the `uri`, an optional `mimeType`, and either `text` or a base64 `blob`","D. The raw HTTP body of the resource, outside the JSON-RPC envelope"],a:2,
e:"`resources/read` returns a **`contents`** array. Each entry echoes the `uri`, may carry a `mimeType`, and holds either **`text`** for textual data or a base64 **`blob`** for binary. A server **MAY** return several entries for one read — reading a directory resource might return multiple files. Two details worth carrying: a non-existent resource must be a JSON-RPC error (`-32602`) rather than an empty `contents` array, and entries may carry annotations (`audience`, `priority`, `lastModified`) that help a host decide what to put into context and in what order.",
w:"A — the field is `contents` (tool results use `content`), and binary resources are fully supported through `blob`; nothing forces them into tools. B — the `uri` and the optional `mimeType` travel with the data precisely so that clients do not have to guess from a file extension. D — every MCP message is a JSON-RPC envelope on both bindings; there is no out-of-band body, and on stdio there is no HTTP at all."},

{id:2033,d:"D5",s:"MCP Server Development",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"MCP also defines primitives a **client** may offer to a server. Which statement reflects the current specification?",
o:["A. Sampling, roots and elicitation are all current, and a server reaches them by sending its own JSON-RPC requests to the client","B. Only sampling is defined; roots and elicitation were proposals that were never adopted","C. All three were removed, and servers must now integrate directly with a model provider","D. Elicitation is current, while sampling and roots are deprecated as of the latest revision; and a server no longer sends its own request — it returns an input-required result whose `inputRequests` the client fulfils when it retries the original request"],a:3,
e:"**Currency note — this moved recently.** Conceptually the three client-side primitives are **sampling** (`sampling/createMessage`, the server borrowing the client's model), **roots** (`roots/list`, the directories the client considers relevant) and **elicitation** (`elicitation/create`, asking the user for input). As of the latest revision **sampling and roots are deprecated** — new implementations SHOULD NOT adopt them, and deprecated features remain in the spec for at least twelve months before becoming eligible for removal. Elicitation remains current, in `form` and `url` modes. Structurally, servers no longer initiate JSON-RPC requests at all: they return an input-required result carrying `inputRequests`, and the client answers by retrying with `inputResponses`.",
w:"A — the delivery mechanism described is the older model; in the current revision servers do not initiate requests on any binding, and two of the three primitives are deprecated. B — roots and elicitation were both specified and shipped; elicitation is the one that remains current. C — only sampling and roots are deprecated, and even they remain in the specification during the deprecation window; the suggested migration for sampling is indeed to integrate with a provider directly, but elicitation is unaffected."}
);
